WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.169 --> 00:00:07.819
I'm standing outside the Bank Of New Zealand
at Cenotaph Corner in Rangiora.

00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:13.440
We're looking at an initiative of the Rangiora
Community Board, now the Rangiora-Ashley Community

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.449
Board to beautify the town centre as a result
of the earthquakes and the street upgrades

00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:19.729
that have gone on.

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:26.699
One of my favourite pictures here is of the
market day in 1910.

00:00:26.699 --> 00:00:31.710
What appeals to me about that, is all the
gigs stacked up and for those of you that

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:36.620
understand farming in those days, the farmers
would go off to the market, have a successful

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:42.150
sale, have a few noggins and my mind just
boggles as they try to untangle the web of

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.730
gigs at the end of the day.

00:00:45.289 --> 00:00:48.429
This is another picture that I find really
interesting.

00:00:48.429 --> 00:00:52.829
This is the Junction Hotel, nattily known
as Robbie’s.

00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:58.719
It was originally damaged in the 1922 earthquakes
and it lost the upper part of the parapet.

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:04.589
It was further damaged in the 2010-2011 earthquakes,
but it has been saved, the front has been

00:01:04.589 --> 00:01:09.259
saved and the rest of the building demolished,
so it's an important part of the Rangiora

00:01:09.259 --> 00:01:11.450
history.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:17.030
This picture is of Worth elephants in 1951,
taken as they were parading down the High

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.329
Street on their way to Dudley Park.

00:01:19.329 --> 00:01:23.570
What I think's important about this picture,
is it's something we'll never ever see in

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:27.610
Rangiora again as circus animals are really
a thing of the past.

00:01:30.359 --> 00:01:35.090
I hope you found these pictures interesting;
they depict much of the history of Rangiora.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:39.690
If you'd like to see more of them, they can
be seen at the Rangiora Museum in Good Street.

